{
"dur": 44.93,
"shots": [
{
"i": 1,
"start": 0.0,
"vo_start": 0.6,
"vo_end": 9.503,
"text": "Digging began in April 1859. At first, there were almost no machines. The work was done by Egyptian farmers, forced to come, around twenty thousand at a time.",
"end": 9.778
},
{
"i": 2,
"start": 9.778,
"vo_start": 10.053,
"vo_end": 16.932,
"text": "They dug with picks, carried sand in baskets on their backs, and fresh water came by camel, or by boat along a new canal from the Nile.",
"end": 17.207
},
{
"i": 3,
"start": 17.207,
"vo_start": 17.482,
"vo_end": 27.682,
"text": "In 1865 cholera swept through the camps. Thousands died. Nobody knows the total death toll of the whole project. Estimates run from several thousand to over a hundred thousand.",
"end": 27.957
},
{
"i": 4,
"start": 27.957,
"vo_start": 28.232,
"vo_end": 35.231,
"text": "Meanwhile, Britain pushed the Ottoman sultan, Egypt's overlord, to ban the forced labour. In 1864 it was stopped.",
"end": 35.506
},
{
"i": 5,
"start": 35.506,
"vo_start": 35.781,
"vo_end": 43.98,
"text": "Overnight the work camps emptied. Half-dug trenches, no workers, money running out. Many in Europe thought the canal was dead.",
"end": 44.93
}
],
